The argument forms around diag.doproc_args_not_array_literal that were run
on STARLIMS 12. With a literal name, a comma with no argument after it, an
empty slot followed by another argument, and a variable in parentheses all
stop the script from compiling, like an array in a variable. With the name
in a variable the comma with no array raises when the call runs. An array
literal in parentheses, commas after the array, a name built by an
expression, a dotted name and ExecFunction all run, so each stays
unreported.
-- input.ssl --
:PROCEDURE RunChecks;
:PARAMETERS aArgs, sKind, sName;
:DECLARE aResult;
aResult := DoProc("CheckEqual",);
aResult := DoProc("CheckEqual",, sKind);
aResult := DoProc("CheckEqual", (aArgs));
aResult := DoProc(sName,);
aResult := DoProc("CheckEqual", ({aArgs[1], aArgs[2]}));
aResult := DoProc("CheckEqual", {aArgs[1], aArgs[2]},);
aResult := DoProc("Check" + sKind, aArgs);
aResult := DoProc("Common.Checks.CheckEqual", aArgs);
aResult := ExecFunction("Common.Checks.CheckEqual", aArgs);
aResult := DoProc("CheckEqual");
:RETURN aResult;
:ENDPROC;

:PROCEDURE CheckEqual;
:PARAMETERS vExpected, vActual;
:RETURN vExpected == vActual;
:ENDPROC;
-- format --
:PROCEDURE RunChecks;
	:PARAMETERS aArgs, sKind, sName;
	:DECLARE aResult;

	aResult := DoProc("CheckEqual",);
	aResult := DoProc("CheckEqual",, sKind);
	aResult := DoProc("CheckEqual", (aArgs));
	aResult := DoProc(sName,);
	aResult := DoProc("CheckEqual", ({aArgs[1], aArgs[2]}));
	aResult := DoProc("CheckEqual", {aArgs[1], aArgs[2]},);
	aResult := DoProc("Check" + sKind, aArgs);
	aResult := DoProc("Common.Checks.CheckEqual", aArgs);
	aResult := ExecFunction("Common.Checks.CheckEqual", aArgs);
	aResult := DoProc("CheckEqual");

	:RETURN aResult;
:ENDPROC;

:PROCEDURE CheckEqual;
	:PARAMETERS vExpected, vActual;

	:RETURN vExpected == vActual;
:ENDPROC;
-- diagnostics --
4:31-4:32 error doproc_args_not_array_literal: DoProc("CheckEqual",) has a comma and no argument array after it, which is a compile-time error with a literal procedure name: write DoProc("CheckEqual") or DoProc("CheckEqual", {...}).
5:31-5:32 error doproc_args_not_array_literal: DoProc("CheckEqual",, ...) has no argument array in its second position, which is a compile-time error with a literal procedure name: write DoProc("CheckEqual") or DoProc("CheckEqual", {...}).
5:34-5:39 warning builtin_excess_arguments: 'DoProc' accepts at most 2 argument(s) - the compiler silently drops the surplus 1 argument (never evaluated)
6:33-6:40 error doproc_args_not_array_literal: DoProc with a literal procedure name takes its arguments as an array literal: write DoProc("CheckEqual", {...}). An array held in a variable or built by an expression is a compile-time error here.
7:24-7:25 error doproc_args_not_array_literal: DoProc(...,) has a comma and no argument array after it, which raises an error when the call runs: drop the comma, or pass the array.
